{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788384773,"nonce":"p+EU/4Hch31/Mt/Qu9Q4ttkT30A=","message":"Third piece, raised by Dorian and NOT covered by the issue body or comment 1\nabove. Recording it here because it is the same workflow, but it is a change to\n`advance`, not to a gizmo verb — whoever implements this should know both ends\nare needed or the feature is half-built.\n\n\nPressing a gizmo that starts a long, multi-tick process gives the caller no way\nto wait for it to FINISH. The bestowing ceremony ran 5,000 ticks\n(505,748 \"ceremony has begun\" -\u003e 510,747 title granted). During that window\nthere is nothing to do and nothing to decide, and the loop has no matcher that\nsays \"wake me when this specific thing is done\".\n\nIt worked this once only by luck: the ritual ends with a `RitualOutcomePositive`\nLETTER, and the advance happened to be on `--until.letter`. That is not a\nsolution — it fails the moment the run needs to ride past unrelated letters\nwhile waiting for the ceremony, which is the normal case on a busy map.\n\n\n`advance --until.layout ly-1` halts when every element of one `place-layout`\ntransaction is built or cancelled. That is exactly the right idea: a matcher\nkeyed to a NAMED, CALLER-INITIATED transaction rather than to an event class.\nThe `until` set is currently `{ticks | letter | threat | alert | event |\ncondition | layout}` — `layout` is the only transaction-scoped one.\n\nProposed siblings:\n\n    advance --until.ritual \u003critual id\u003e     halts when the ritual resolves,\n                                           whatever its outcome\n    advance --until.quest \u003cquest id\u003e       halts when the quest leaves Ongoing\n    advance --until.gizmo \u003chandle\u003e         halts when the flow that handle\n                                           started completes or aborts\n\nThe general rule worth pinning: **anything the agent can START and that takes\nmore than one tick should be waitable by name.** Otherwise the caller either\npolls (burning turns and re-reading digests for nothing) or over-broadens the\nmatcher and stops on things it did not care about.\n\n\nA gizmo verb without this is not usable unattended: you press \"begin ceremony\",\nand then you are back to guessing how long to advance. A caller that guesses\nshort polls; a caller that guesses long rides past the raid that arrived\nmid-ritual. The three pieces — read the gizmos, press one and answer its steps,\nwait for the result — are one feature.\n\n\n- Every gizmo press that starts a multi-tick process returns a handle, and\n  `advance --until.\u003cthat handle\u003e` halts when it resolves.\n- The halt payload says the OUTCOME (granted / failed / cancelled), not just\n  that it ended.\n- Waiting on a named transaction does not suppress the safety halts: a threat,\n  a casualty or a red error still stops the advance, exactly as they do under\n  `--until.layout` today.","files":null}]}